  Frederick points out being a student is  different than being in uniform many of  us getting out of military that's that  was a big chunk of our lives so we lose  that part that our sense of being part  of something so we go to school we  become part of something else  it's completely foreign in a way in a  way yes the government spends 12 billion  dollars a year helping veterans pay for  college through the GI Bill but  currently 40% of that goes to for-profit  colleges post 9-11 veterans barely make  up 1% of the nation's elite colleges and  universities ambers professor Jeffrey  Sanborn spent time with the perspective  students he says colleges like his need  more veterans on campus their military  background is like a source of strength  in this context you know it's not  something that you have to kind of like  check at the door the program encourages  vets like 21 year old Tracey Santos to  consider community college with an eye  on eventually transferring to a top  school after first learning how to be a  student again I think the scariest part  is that these people that I am now  surrounded by have come from school it's  like I've been out of school for four  and a half years and it's like starting  over  Santos joined the Marines right after  high school she says one key to the  program is it's taught by veterans they  know exactly how to talk to you and they  put what you've done before in the  service to what you can be doing now  bucks spent 11 years in the Army  including three tours in Iraq and went  through the warrior Scholar Program  himself in 2015 now he uses his personal  story to remind the students of what's  important I have eight friends tattooed  on my arm that have died but only three  of them actually died in combat the  other five went out due to demons in  their own head five new friends yeah and  there's actually more than that now  fueled by that loss buck is working  toward medical school where he hopes to  become a psychiatrist to 